entries.body:
A program can now allocate into a region of its own and free it, all at once. `import { Region, region, regionWith, enter, enterWith, free } from "nish:region"`:
- `region(f)` and `regionWith(arg, f)` make a region whose root is what `f` answers;
- `enter` and `enterWith` run a function in it again;
- `free` gives all of its memory back.
A `Region<T>` is a copied i64 handle, so a long-running server can bound its memory to the live set: one region per session, freed when the session ends (docs/wp46-long-running-memory.md).

The checker refuses every value that would outlive its region, NL2609 to NL2618:
- region memory stored into outer memory, and outer memory stored into a region;
- an `enter` that answers anything but a number, boolean, enum or handle;
- a handle used on, or sent to, another thread;
- a root that is not fresh;
- outer storage grown inside a region;
- `Arena.mark` or `Arena.used` reached from inside one;
- `new Region`.
The rule follows every function an `f` reaches. That includes each arrow handed to a higher-order function, whose parameters receive everything the callee can see, the answers of the other arrows in the same call among them. It runs to a fixpoint. `--emit-ts` refuses a program that uses regions (NL4015). A program that does not import `nish:region` compiles to the same IR as before.

This also settles three runtime findings from stage 1:
- RT-45: a mark taken in one region cannot be released in another;
- RT-46: the region table's lock is released before a panic;
- RT-47: a region's chunk doubling stops at 1 MiB.
